  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=6{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe acceleration is $150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(18{t}^{2}+6t) \\\\  & =36t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=150,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150 & =36t+6 \\\\ 36t & =144 \\\\ t & =4\\text{\u00a0}\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is $150\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at $t=4$ seconds.",
